Title: The Lamp in the Attic
Credit: Written by
Author: Andy Example
Source: A short film rolled with storywheel
Draft date: 5 October 2026

/* A short sample script for storywheel's tests and for trying the export. It uses every element the Writer and the PDF know. */

FADE IN:

# Act One

= Mara comes back to her mother's house to sell it, and finds the attic lamp still burning.

EXT. HOLLOWAY HOUSE - NIGHT

Rain hammers a tall, narrow house at the end of a lane. Every window is dark except one: a small round window under the roof, where a lamp burns.

MARA HOLLOWAY (40s, coat soaked through, a ring of keys in her fist) stands at the gate.

MARA (V.O.)
I told myself I would never come back. Not for the funeral. Not for the house. Not for anything that was left in it.

She opens the gate. It shrieks.

INT. HOLLOWAY HOUSE - HALLWAY - CONTINUOUS

Dust sheets over everything. Mara finds a light switch. Nothing.

MARA
(under her breath)
Of course.

VOICE (O.S.)
Close the door, Mara. You're letting the cold in.

Mara freezes. Turns slowly toward the stairs.

MARA
Who's there?

Silence. Then, very faintly, a door closes somewhere above her.

MARA (CONT'D)
I have a phone. I'm calling the police.

She does not take out her phone.

INT. HOLLOWAY HOUSE - ATTIC - NIGHT

A low room under the eaves. Boxes, a rocking horse, a dressmaker's dummy. In the middle of the floor, on a tea chest, the lamp: brass, polished, burning steadily.

Beside it sits BRAM ASHER (70s, cardigan, carpet slippers), the old neighbor. He doesn't look surprised to see her.

BRAM
You took your time.

MARA ^
What are you doing in my mother's house?

BRAM
Keeping the lamp lit. Same as every night for thirty-one years. She asked me to, the week your father left, and I said I would, and I'm not a man who stops doing a thing just because the person who asked him has gone and died on him. You'd know that if you'd ever once come home. Every night at nine I come up those stairs, I wipe the glass, I trim the wick, and I sit with it until it's steady. And every night I think, this is the night Mara comes back. Thirty-one years of nights. So yes. You took your time.

Mara sits down on a box. The fight goes out of her.

MARA
Why the lamp?

BRAM
(a long look at her)
So you'd know where the house was. In the dark.

CUT TO:

> THE NEXT MORNING <

===

# Act Two

= The house won't let her sell it, and she starts to understand why.

EXT. HOLLOWAY HOUSE - GARDEN - DAY

Wet grass. A FOR SALE sign leans against the wall. Mara, in last night's clothes, drinks coffee from a jar.

An ESTATE AGENT (30s, relentlessly cheerful) picks his way across the lawn.

ESTATE AGENT
Lovely bones. Lovely, lovely bones. We'll want the attic cleared, of course. Buyers don't like to see a lamp burning in an empty house. Gives them ideas.

MARA
What ideas?

ESTATE AGENT
(laughing)
That somebody's still home.

Mara looks up. The round window. The lamp, burning in daylight.

INT. HOLLOWAY HOUSE - ATTIC - DAY

Mara alone with the lamp. She reaches for it, hesitates, and turns the little brass key that lowers the wick.

The flame does not go down.

She turns the key the whole way. The flame stays exactly where it is.

MARA
Mum?

The lamp flickers once.

MARA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
I'm here. I came back.

~ Hush now, the lamp is lit,
~ the house is warm, and you are in it.

She knows the song. She sings the next line under her breath without meaning to.

DISSOLVE TO:

INT. HOLLOWAY HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Bram and Mara at the kitchen table, a bottle of whisky between them.

BRAM
She never blamed you.

MARA
She should have.

BRAM
Well. She didn't.

> FADE OUT.
